==============================================================================
HAND-WRITTEN POLICY -- decisions made by a rule nobody learned
==============================================================================

==============================================================================
WHAT TO REMOVE FIRST
==============================================================================
  1. `walls` / `hazards` -- the biggest lie. He is OFFERED only legal
     moves, so he can never walk into a wall and learn one. Replace
     the offer with an attempt that can FAIL: the move is tried, the
     world refuses it, and the refusal is the percept. That is the
     'know it after colliding with it' Nick asked for, and it is the
     one change that makes the others measurable.
  2. `ghosts` -- perfect position knowledge through walls at any range.
     Needs a sensor with a range and a line of sight; until one exists
     the threat layer is simulated omniscience. Which is why the first
     levels should run WITH NO GHOSTS AT ALL (Nick): learning the
     environment and surviving an omniscient threat model at once
     produces neither.
  3. `remaining` / `power` / `reach` -- the solved map. He should know
     only the cells he has stood in and what he saw from them.
  4. the hand-written policies -- once 1-3 are percepts rather than
     reads, a policy written against ground truth cannot run anyway,
     so they fall out rather than needing removal.

  Order matters: fixing the policy first would leave it reading an
  oracle; fixing the oracle first makes the policy fail loudly.